## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of runtime configurability, deduplication logic, and offset management—skills essential for building robust streaming pipelines in production systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Runtime config updates**: Using a dedicated configuration topic to dynamically control sink behavior
- **Per-message deduplication**: Maintaining deduplication state across message processing
- **Toggle behavior**: Safely switching deduplication on/off without losing in-flight messages
- **At-least-once semantics**: Ensuring offsets commit even when deduplication is bypassed
- **Graceful state management**: Handling the transition between dedup-enabled and disabled modes

## Code Example
```javascript
class RuntimeConfig {
  constructor() { this.disableDedup = false; }

  apply(event) {
    if (typeof event.disableDedup === 'boolean') {
      this.disableDedup = event.disableDedup;
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions between config updates and incoming messages?
- What happens to deduplication state when the connector restarts?
- How would you implement this using Kafka Streams instead of a sink connector?

## Why This Is Asked

To test understanding of consumer rebalances and offset management, a common pitfall for beginners where duplicates occur if offsets aren't tracked during rebalances.

## Key Concepts

- Consumer group rebalancing
- Per-topic offset tracking
- In-flight state
- Safe commit on revoke
- Timeouts and fault tolerance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo code showing API hooks
class RebalanceSafeConsumer {
  constructor() { this.committedOffsets = new Map(); this.inFlight = new Set(); }
  onPartitionsAssigned(parts) { for (const p of parts) { const k = `${p.topic}:${p.partition}`; const start = (this.committedOffsets.get(k) ?? -1) + 1; this.seek(p.topic, p.partition, start); } }
  onPartitionsRevoked(parts) { const revoked = new Set(parts.map(p => `${p.topic}:${p.partition}`)); /* wait for inFlight on revoked before committing */ }
  processMessage(msg) { const id = `${msg.topic}:${msg.partition}:${msg.offset}`; this.inFlight.add(id); // downstream write then:
    this.inFlight.delete(id); const key = `${msg.topic}:${msg.partition}`; this.committedOffsets.set(key, msg.offset); }
  commitOffsets() { /* commit logic here */ }
}
```

